    "WORK-EFFORT": {
      "type": "feature",
      "requires": [],
      "verify": "npx vitest run conformance/doc-audit/work-ranking.test.mjs",
      "added": "2026-08-07",
      "notes": "Within a class, ranking falls through unblock-count and added-date to alphabetical ID, so it cannot tell a 5-file fix from a 164-file campaign. The imported ratchet items make this concrete: no-floating-promises (5 files) and no-unnecessary-condition (164 files) rank adjacently on identical signals. Options: an explicit effort field on the item, or deriving one from the file count work:import already computes. Whichever lands, add the ordering rule to the ranking tests and to the 'why this one' output."
    },
    "WORK-UNATTENDED": {
      "type": "feature",
      "requires": [],
      "verify": "npx vitest run conformance/doc-audit/work-ranking.test.mjs",
      "added": "2026-08-07",
      "notes": "The nine RQ soaks are not nine decisions: they are one action (npm run validate:mac -- --stage 8 --plan-duration) followed by up to 72 hours of waiting. work:next allocates attention, but its top answer currently costs almost none, and it presents the batch as a queue to pick from. Marking such items unattended would let work:next say 'start these, then do this' and rank the hands-on item second. Relates to work:done --from-log, which already assumes soaks run outside the tool."
